diff --git a/docs/self-hosting.md b/docs/self-hosting.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..32f6fcb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/self-hosting.md
@@ -0,0 +1,162 @@
+## Self-hosting
+
+Fizzy ships with a Kamal configuration for running it on a single box: one Linux
+server with Docker, SQLite and everything in one container, with SSL provided by
+the proxy. No database server, no queue, no registry account.
+
+You need:
+
+- A Linux server reachable over SSH, with ports 80 and 443 open. Kamal installs
+  Docker on it if it's missing, so a fresh box is fine. Sign in as `root` or as
+  a user who can run `docker`.
+- Docker on the machine you deploy from, to build the image.
+- A hostname with a DNS record pointing at the server. Let's Encrypt needs it to
+  issue the certificate.
+- A mail server you can send from. Fizzy signs people in with magic links, so
+  without working email delivery there's no way in.
+
+### Fill in your hostname
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml` and replace the `fizzy.example.com` placeholder, or set
+`APP_HOST` in your environment instead:
+
+```sh
+export APP_HOST=fizzy.yourdomain.com
+```
+
+The hostname is used for the Docker container, the proxy and the links Fizzy
+puts in its emails.
+
+### Fill in your mail server
+
+Still in `config/deploy.yml`, point the `SMTP_*` variables at your mail server:
+
+```yaml
+env:
+  clear:
+    SMTP_ADDRESS: smtp.mailprovider.com
+    SMTP_PORT: 587
+    SMTP_AUTHENTICATION: plain
+    SMTP_USER_NAME: fizzy@mailprovider.com
+```
+
+Add `SMTP_USER_NAME` only if your mail server requires authentication. The
+password goes in your secrets file, not in the config:
+
+```
+SMTP_PASSWORD=…
+```
+
+Set `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` if you don't want Fizzy to send as
+`Fizzy <no-reply@your-host>`.
+
+### Fill in your secrets
+
+Copy the secrets template and fill it in. `.kamal/secrets` is git-ignored, so it
+stays on your machine.
+
+```sh
+cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+```
+
+Fill in `SECRET_KEY_BASE` — generate one with `bin/rails secret` — and the mail
+password. The VAPID keys are only needed if you want browser push
+notifications; if you skip them, remove those lines here and from
+`config/deploy.yml`.
+
+### Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+This installs Docker on your server, builds the image, pushes it, boots the app
+behind [kamal-proxy](https://github.com/basecamp/kamal-proxy), and runs the
+migrations. Point your browser at `https://your-host` and create your account
+through the sign-up form.
+
+Kamal stops with a message when the placeholders are still in `config/deploy.yml`,
+so a deploy can't go out half-configured.
+
+Deploys after that are `bin/kamal deploy`.
+
+### What runs where
+
+Everything lives in one container, `fizzy-web`:
+
+- Puma serves the app, behind [Thruster](https://github.com/basecamp/thruster) —
+  `kamal-proxy` sits in front of both and terminates SSL, redirecting HTTP to
+  HTTPS.
+- Solid Queue runs background jobs inside Puma. Recurring tasks from
+  `config/recurring.yml` run on the box too.
+- Solid Cache and Solid Cable keep cache and Action Cable in SQLite.
+
+All of the SQLite databases and uploaded files live under `/rails/storage`,
+which is mounted from a Docker volume named `fizzy`. Replacing the container on
+a deploy leaves the volume — and your data — alone.
+
+Take backups of it like any other Docker volume:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app stop
+docker run --rm -v fizzy:/data -v "$PWD":/backup alpine \
+  tar czf /backup/fizzy-storage.tgz -C /data .
+bin/kamal app start
+```
+
+Stopping the app first gives you a consistent snapshot of the SQLite databases.
+
+### Day to day
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy             # Ship a new version
+bin/kamal logs               # Tail the app logs
+bin/kamal console            # Rails console on the server
+bin/kamal ssh                # Shell into the container
+bin/kamal rollback <version> # Back out a bad deploy
+```
+
+Kamal keeps a few old images around, so a rollback doesn't need a rebuild.
+
+### Options
+
+**Jobs in their own container.** Set `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: "false"` in the
+`env.clear` section and add a role to `servers`:
+
+```yaml
+servers:
+  web:
+    hosts:
+      - <%= host %>
+  jobs:
+    hosts:
+      - <%= host %>
+    cmd: bin/jobs
+```
+
+**A real registry.** By default the image is built on your machine and pushed to
+a throwaway registry, which the server pulls from over an SSH tunnel. To use
+Docker Hub or ghcr.io instead, replace the `registry` section:
+
+```yaml
+registry:
+  server: ghcr.io
+  username: your-user
+  password:
+    - KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD
+```
+
+…with `KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD` in `.kamal/secrets`.
+
+**Other architectures.** `builder.arch` is set to `amd64`, which is what most
+servers are. Remove it if you deploy from a machine with the same architecture
+as your server.
+
+**SSL from somewhere else.** kamal-proxy handles the certificate. If you put
+your own load balancer in front of the box instead, set `proxy.ssl: false` and
+`DISABLE_SSL: "true"` in `env.clear`, so Fizzy stops assuming it is being
+reached over HTTPS.
+
+**More than one account.** Fizzy runs as a single-account instance by default.
+Set `MULTI_TENANT: "true"` in `env.clear` to let anyone sign up for their own
+account. Accounts are then kept apart by a prefix in the URL path.
diff --git a/test/self_hosting_config_test.rb b/test/self_hosting_config_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..793ae71
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/self_hosting_config_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "kamal"
+
+class SelfHostingConfigTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  HOST = "fizzy.example.com"
+
+  setup do
+    @original_host = ENV["APP_HOST"]
+    ENV["APP_HOST"] = HOST
+    @config = Kamal::Configuration.create_from(config_file: Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"))
+  end
+
+  teardown do
+    if @original_host
+      ENV["APP_HOST"] = @original_host
+    else
+      ENV.delete("APP_HOST")
+    end
+  end
+
+  test "deploys to a single box" do
+    assert_equal [ "web" ], @config.roles.map(&:name)
+    assert_equal [ HOST ], @config.all_hosts
+  end
+
+  test "uses the hostname everywhere" do
+    assert_equal HOST, @config.primary_host
+    assert_equal [ HOST ], @config.primary_role.proxy.hosts
+    assert_equal HOST, @config.env.clear["APP_HOST"]
+  end
+
+  test "terminates SSL at the proxy" do
+    assert @config.primary_role.proxy.ssl?
+  end
+
+  test "keeps the data on a volume" do
+    assert_includes @config.volume_args.join(" "), "/rails/storage"
+  end
+
+  test "reads the image from a registry" do
+    assert @config.registry.server.present?
+    assert @config.repository.end_with?(@config.image)
+  end
+
+  test "runs background jobs on the box" do
+    assert_equal "true", @config.env.clear["SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA"]
+  end
+
+  test "configures the mail server" do
+    assert_equal "smtp.example.com", @config.env.clear["SMTP_ADDRESS"]
+    assert_includes @config.env.secret_keys, "SMTP_PASSWORD"
+  end
+
+  test "documents every secret in the template" do
+    template_keys = secrets_example.map { |line| line[/\A([A-Z_]+)=/, 1] }.compact
+
+    @config.env.secret_keys.each do |secret|
+      assert_includes template_keys, secret, ".kamal/secrets.example is missing #{secret}"
+    end
+  end
+
+  private
+    def secrets_example
+      File.readlines(Rails.root.join(".kamal/secrets.example")).grep(/\A[A-Z_]+=/)
+    end
+end
